[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 6:23] I don't have waste or shrink figures, and I don't have product mix by volume. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit.

[candidate · 7:51] That reconciles. Two years ago COGS was 42 points of revenue: beans 25% of that is 10.5 points, other inputs 31.5 points. Beans up 40% gives 14.7; other inputs up 37.5% gives 43.3. Sum is 58 points — exactly today's COGS. So it's input cost inflation across the board, not waste, and not something idiosyncratic to coffee.

Also worth noting: ticket is up 10% to $6.80 on flat prices, so revenue growth is customers buying more items, not more traffic. That's volume-driven and doesn't help margin when every unit costs more.

So the recommendation stands, with the pricing lever better supported now: costs are up ~38% blended while price is unchanged.

I'm done — can we do another case?
